<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Palace State of Malia</h1><p class="st-meta">1950 BCE – 1450 BCE (approx.) · capital Malia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Minoan palace centre on Crete's north coast, one of the island's great Bronze Age palaces and the source of the famous gold bee pendant.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Malia grew from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-minoan-period">Early Minoan</a> settlement on a fertile coastal plain east of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palace-state-of-knossos">Knossos</a>. Around 1950 BCE, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protopalatial-period">Protopalatial period</a>, a monumental palace was built around a central court, making it one of the earliest palace centres on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> alongside Knossos and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palace-state-of-phaistos">Phaistos</a>. The palace was destroyed c. 1700 BCE, probably by earthquake, and rebuilt on a similar plan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers are unknown, as Minoan scripts remain undeciphered, but the palace stored grain, oil and wine in large pithoi and organised craft production. The nearby Quartier Mu workshops produced seals, pottery and metalwork, and the Chrysolakkos burial complex yielded the gold pendant of two bees. Inscriptions in Cretan hieroglyphic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> show an administrative system shared with other palaces.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Like most Cretan palaces, Malia was destroyed by fire c. 1450 BCE, in the wave of destructions that preceded Mycenaean dominance from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palace-state-of-knossos">Knossos</a>. It was not rebuilt as a palace, though limited settlement continued. Excavated from 1915 and by the French School at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> since 1922, it is among the best-preserved Minoan sites.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1950 BCE – 1450 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:20.90%;width:9.95%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1950 BCE – 1450 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century-bce">20th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Malia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Minoan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Minoan (undeciphered)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Malia, Mallia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1700&amp;focus=palace-state-of-malia" aria-label="Palace State of Malia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5324 902 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Palace State of Malia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5644 1158h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5644 1158h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Malia. <a href="/atlas?year=-1700&amp;focus=palace-state-of-malia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

